-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### A1.2 Creating Storage Credentials
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Creating storage credentials is done through the **Catalog Explorer** user interface (this functionality is also available through the API).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. From **Catalog Explorer**, select **Connect &gt; Credentials**.
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Credentials** tab, click **Create credential**.
-- MAGIC 1. With **Storage Credential** selected, select the **Credential Type** (in this case, *AWS IAM Role*).
-- MAGIC 1. Fill in a unique **Credential name**.
-- MAGIC 1. Fill in the **IAM role (ARN)** field.
-- MAGIC 1. Click **Create**.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       When connecting to new storage locations, you must update the trust relationship in your cloud provider to allow for the Unity Catalog control plane. The Databricks UI provides additional instructions on what needs to be configured.
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### A2. External Locations
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC External locations map storage credentials to specific cloud storage paths. They serve as the foundation for the following important capabilities:
-- MAGIC - **Managed storage overrides:** catalogs (or schemas) can route all data associated with their managed tables or volumes to external cloud storage, allowing for physical isolation at the catalog or schema level
-- MAGIC - **External tables and volumes:** tables and volumes can individually host their data in external cloud storage
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Since external locations can be applied to a path within the storage container, we can associate multiple external locations to a single storage credential.
-- MAGIC
