-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### B2.  External Tables/Volumes
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Databricks can provide governance over data living in external cloud storage at the level of tables (structured data) or files (unstructured data) using external tables or volumes.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Common use cases include:
-- MAGIC - **Registering existing data without migration:** if you have data files already stored in cloud object storage, you can create an external table or volume pointing to those files without moving data. This is especially useful duriing migration from HMS and for replacing legacy DBFS mounts.
-- MAGIC - **Interoperability with Non-Databricks Systems:** if downstream or upstream workflows require non-Databricks tools to directly read or write to those files, external tables are recommended. 
-- MAGIC - **File Formats:** when you need to register tables that use file formats not supported by Unity Catalog managed tables (like Parquet, Avro, ORC, or plain text), external tables provide flexibility for various data types and schemas. 
-- MAGIC - **Data Isolation:** similar to how we can isolate data at the catalog or schema level, external tables/volumes provides isolation at the level of tables/volumes.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### B2.1 Create an External Table/Volume
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In a SQL editor or notebook, execute the following SQL code to create an external volume.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div class="code-block" data-language="sql">
-- MAGIC CREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME dev
-- MAGIC   'LOCATION {storage container base URL}/dev'
-- MAGIC </div>
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
